The thesis models the discharge of an 18650 lithium-ion cell for portable communication equipment. It starts from the electrode reactions and derives an equivalent circuit with a single polarization branch whose parameters depend on temperature through the Arrhenius equation. The model is extended by Bernardi's heat balance and solved numerically with the fourth-order Runge–Kutta method. Parameters were identified from discharge measurements at currents from 0.5C to 2C and temperatures from $-20$ to $45$ °C. The root mean square error of the terminal voltage stayed below 25 mV in all validation scenarios. The thesis closes with recommendations for operating the power sources at low temperatures. All data and results are fictitious and serve only to demonstrate the thesis template.
